# CHANGELOG

## 20260808

0.14.0: + --private, for the Github repositories which should not be public.

1. + --private, as -p, passed through to create_repository. TODO item 1, which has been sitting there since the Github path was written.
2. + The switch is declared with boolean rather than set, so as it takes no argument and does not swallow the URI which follows it. An unsupplied boolean reads as nil rather than false and the API wants the field either way, so it is made a boolean at the call rather than passed as it comes.
3. Bears upon the Github path alone. A bare repository made over ssh is as private as the machine it sits on, and there is nothing there to ask for.
4. + Three tests, of what can be tested without the API and a token: that --private and -p are each taken as a switch rather than as the repository to create, and that neither swallows the URI which follows. An undeclared switch is left in ARGV, and ARGV[0] is read as the repository to create, so a switch which is not properly declared would have git-boot try to create a repository called --private.
5. The access token must carry the whole repo scope, public_repo alone being refused for a private repository. Noted in the README, since the failure is a 403 from the API rather than anything git-boot can see coming.

## 20260807

0.13.0: Work in a repository which already exists.

1. + has_commits?, and create_local_repo returns upon it. What is booted is a directory with no commits, which is not the same as one with no .git: git init was already conditional, but create_root_commit was not, so in a repository with history and no .gitignore it made a commit named '+ .gitignore' upon the tip. That commit is not a root commit, has a parent, and was never asked for. A directory which has been git init'd and left alone still has no commits and so still gets the whole treatment, which is the case the guard has to leave alone.
2. + ensure_origin_is_free, called before anything remote is made. push_to_remote_repo ran git remote add origin and then pushed to origin without looking at whether the add had succeeded, so where origin was already there the add failed and the push went to whatever origin had been pointing at all along. Asking for a repository at one address and quietly sending the code to another is the worst of what was here.
3. + The refusal precedes create_remote_github_repo rather than following it, so as nothing is left half done: no empty repository on the far end, and no remote pointing anywhere it was not told to. How many remotes a repository keeps is its own business, git being distributed; the only thing which cannot be allowed is pushing to one which was not named on the command line.
4. ~ push_to_remote_repo: git push --follow-tags, in place of git push. A repository being booted has no tags to lose, but one which already exists generally does, and leaving them behind makes the remote a poorer copy than the local.
5. ~ push_to_remote_repo: system with an argument list, in place of a string with a URI interpolated into it.
6. + Six tests over the two new refusals and the case they serve: no commit added where there is history, untracked files left untracked, no .gitignore conjured, the refusal naming what origin points at, the existing remote undisturbed by it, and no refusal where no remote was asked for at all.
7. + README case 6, for a repository which already exists.

## 20260807

0.12.0: + test/git-boot_test.rb

1. + test/git-boot_test.rb, the first tests of any kind here. git-boot is run as a subprocess in a directory made for the purpose, and what is asserted is the repository it leaves behind rather than anything it printed. With no argument it does the local half and stops, so nothing wants a network, a remote, or a credential, and the remote paths are left uncovered rather than mocked.
2. + Seven checks over the five states a directory can be in when git-boot is run in it: empty, holding files, already a repository, already a repository holding files, and holding nothing but dotfiles. The root commit is asserted to be parentless and to come first, that being the whole reason it is made unconditionally.
3. + The dotfile case is the one which has actually bitten. entries_to_add reads Dir['.*'], which carries '.', '..' and '.git' along with anything wanted, and a .gitignore already in the directory must not earn a second commit of its own, it being the root commit's own file.
4. Nothing was found broken. What 0.11.0 to 0.11.3 arrived at is right in all five states, and this holds it there.

## 20260806

0.11.3: Fill out the .gitignore.

1. ~ .gitignore: from the single .DS_Store line to the whole set for Ruby, being gem and build products, Bundler, RDoc, YARD, editor files, Ruby version managers, test artefacts and .claude. It now matches the one in impuri. Nothing unwanted had reached the repository, .DS_Store having been the one line which mattered and .claude not having existed while any of this history was written, but the rules only ever protect what follows them.

## 20260806

0.11.2: Let the access token chain fall through absence, and stop upon misconfiguration.

1. ~ access_token(): the chain was written as though each source signalled absence by returning nil, and two of the three did not. unencrypted_access_token() raised TypeError where there was no .token file at all, since File.read(nil), and encrypted_access_token() raised where the credential was not found, Credentials.find returning an object whose credentials are nil. || falls through nil and not exceptions, so either aborted the chain rather than passing to the next source.
2. + configured_access_token_name(), unencrypted_access_token() and encrypted_access_token(): a guard apiece, so that a source which is not configured returns nil and is fallen past, while one which is configured and unusable still raises with its own error. Rescuing instead would report a corrupt store as no token found, which is the wrong diagnosis.
3. ~ encrypted_access_token(): three states told apart. The store being unreachable, the key not being to hand, is ordinary and falls through. A named credential the store does not hold stops, naming it being a statement of intent.
4. + access_token(): a message where no source yields a token, rather than passing nil to Octokit and having Github answer 401.

## 20260806

0.11.1: Fix the push to a remote.

1. ~ push_to_remote_repo(): the impuri parameter defaulted to nil, and the branch of main() for a remote which is not Github passes no argument, so it raised NoMethodError upon nil.username. It defaults to self.impuri now. Broken since 0.8.0 in March 2020, so every non-Github remote since.
2. ~ push_to_remote_repo(): git push origin HEAD rather than origin master, git-boot running git init itself, so the branch is whatever init.defaultBranch said at the time rather than master by assumption.

## 20260805

0.11.0: Remove the on-the-fly authorization flow, which Github made impossible in 2020.

1. - authorization() and on_the_fly_access_token(). Both rested upon Octokit::Client.new(login:, password:) and #create_authorization, and Github removed password authentication for the API and the OAuth Authorizations API in 2020, so neither could succeed. A token must now be supplied, stored encrypted, or stored unencrypted.
2. - words() and random_word_for_authorization_note(), which existed only to name the authorization, and with them require File/self.collect.
3. - The -o, --otp switch, there being no longer a request to attach a one time password to. It also read one_time_password rather than switches.one_time_password, and so would have raised NameError rather than sending the header.
4. ~ access_token(): supplied, then encrypted, then unencrypted. The chain no longer ends in something which cannot work.
5. ~ README.md: - the section documenting the on-the-fly flow, and - it from the list of token sources, since neither describes anything which exists now. /ApiCredentials/Credentials/ in that list too, stale since 0.10.0.

## 20260805

0.10.0: Always make the .gitignore commit the root, rename ApiCredentials to Credentials, and vendor the supporting libraries.

1. + create_root_commit(): commits the .gitignore unconditionally, rather than only where there was nothing else to commit. A root commit has no parent, so `git reset --soft HEAD~1` has nothing to land on and `git rebase -i HEAD~n` fails with an unreachable-HEAD error, and rewriting one needs --orphan or --root. Booting a repo in a directory which already held files previously made real work the root, which is the case most likely to want rewriting later.
2. + add_entries(): commits whatever was already there as a second commit rather than as the first.
3. + entries_to_add(): the emptiness test now counts dotfiles, since Dir['*'] does not match them, and so a directory holding only, say, .gitignore and .claude read as empty.
4. ~ create_local_repo(): now reads as init, root commit, then entries.
5. /ApiCredentials/Credentials/, keeping the Credentials version number sequence and retiring ApiCredentials. encrypted_access_token() now calls Credentials.find rather than ApiCredentials.find.
6. + require_gem for impuri, net/ssh, net/ssh/shell, octokit, switches.rb and sym, in place of plain requires, so that a missing gem reports itself rather than failing as a bare LoadError. net/ssh is now named explicitly, having previously been reached only by way of net/ssh/shell, despite create_remote_repo() calling Net::SSH.start directly.
7. ~ The $LOAD_PATH.unshift of lib/ moved above the requires which depend upon it. require 'Kernel/require_gem' had been sitting above it, and so could only ever have been satisfied from elsewhere on the load path rather than from this repository's own lib/.
8. + Vendored the supporting libraries into lib/, being _meta/, Array/, FalseClass/, Hash/, Kernel/, NilClass/, Numeric/, Object/, String/, Thoran/Kernel/ and TrueClass/, which had until now been reached only through RUBYLIB. lib/ goes from 11 files to 44.
9. + Pd::CleartextPasswords and Pd::EncryptedPasswords, separating the two representations of the password store, each knowing how to become the other.
10. ~ Pd::PasswordFile: reduced to reading and writing the file, the encryption having moved to Pd::CleartextPasswords and Pd::EncryptedPasswords. /DEFAULT_ENCRYPTED_FILENAME/DEFAULT_CRYPT_LOCATION/, and - the rescue of RuntimeError which printed and exited, since a library ought not to exit on its caller's behalf.
11. + Pd::Password.reset, to empty the password list.
12. + TODO.txt: Remove the on-the-fly authorization flow, becuase Github removed both password authentication for the API and the OAuth Authorizations API in 2020, so authorization() and on_the_fly_access_token() can no longer succeed.

## 20250702

0.9.0: Introduce encrypted token storage as the preferred method of storing access tokens, while maintaining backward compatibility with unencrypted access tokens.

1. + require 'ApiCredentials', to allow encrypted token storage.
2. /:note, :token_note, :access_token_note/:name, :token_name, :access_token_name/
3. + config_filename(): returns the path to GitHub config file.
4. + configured_access_token_name(): reads and evaluates config file to get ACCESS_TOKEN_NAME.
5. + access_token_name(): determine which token name to use.
6. + encrypted_access_token(): retrieve encrypted token using ApiCredentials.
7. /supplied_stored_access_token_filename()/supplied_unencrypted_access_token_filename()/
8. /default_stored_access_token_filename()/default_unencrypted_access_token_filename()/
9. /stored_access_token_filename()/unencrypted_access_token_filename()/
10. /stored_access_token()/unencrypted_access_token()/
11. ~ access_token(): updated priority order to prefer encrypted tokens over unencrypted, but still after a supplied access token.
